{
  "items": [
    {"n": 1, "name": "First screen", "score": 2, "reason": "The headline, the decision block and the frontier claim tell a reader what is unresolved and what settles it. Nothing declares a price per point. The page gives the bands where the pick flips ($4.15 to $6.11 and $11.09 to $22.05), an Atlas-M lean that is marked as the analyst's own, and the main risk, Brisk-S's drops on the safety checks. I checked the figures against the tool's price read: $44 more for Atlas-L, +2.96 and +5.13 pooled points, and $4.94 and $14.76 per point all hold."},
    {"n": 2, "name": "Coherent story", "score": 1, "reason": "The claims connect: frontier, then safety, then the gap between the two Atlas models, then the Open-70B detail, and they match their tables row by row. One sentence is wrong on the page's own figures. Claim latency calls troubleshooting 'the slowest task', but in the appendix research-needed questions is slower for every model at p95 (Atlas-L 4.86 s vs 4.68 s, Atlas-M 2.84 s vs 2.73 s, Brisk-S 1.22 s vs 1.17 s, Open-70B 2.16 s vs 2.08 s). It is also slower at the median, with Open-70B tied."},
    {"n": 3, "name": "Honest emphasis", "score": 1, "reason": "Atlas-M's weak axis, overall quality against Atlas-L, is plain to see, and the 0% traffic share of speculation bait is flagged. But the decision block, the 'What you could change' table and the frontier caption all say Open-70B is never the best buy, at every price. That holds only on point estimates. Open-70B's pooled lead over Brisk-S is +0.79, with an unpaired interval of -0.19 to +1.77 (price.brisk-s.open-70b.overall.gain_interval_unpaired). So the price per point from Brisk-S to Open-70B runs from $3.11 upward with no top end, which reaches below the $4.15 edge of the Brisk-S/Atlas-M band."},
    {"n": 4, "name": "Sound elision", "score": 2, "reason": "Each omission checks out. Billing alone keeps the same three models on the frontier in the same order. No left-out pass/fail check separates the two Atlas models; tool calls, for example, read +2 with an interval of -1.6 to +5.6. Latency keeps the same order in every task, and no budget was declared. Open-70B's per-task strength in persona small talk is shown in claim open70b and followed up in the next steps."},
    {"n": 5, "name": "Confounds handled", "score": 2, "reason": "Five caveats are carried, each with the condition under which it would matter and a next step: the unstated judge of the pass/fail checks, no control so every gap is unpaired, best of several candidates, nothing declared, and the unnamed provider routing. The cost_served_by fact is met as far as the data allows: the input names no provider, and the page says so in the confidence line, the Watch line, Method and a next step. The high confidence over open caveats is acceptable here, because the page makes no call and each clause says which direction the caveat would push."},
    {"n": 6, "name": "Actionable next steps", "score": 2, "reason": "Each step names an action and what it settles: declaring a price per point or floors, running the routed Open-70B/Atlas-M mix as its own arm, stating and calibrating the pass/fail judge, re-scoring the two Atlas models paired, and naming providers. The figure of 1,555 cases per model is right (settle.atlas-l.atlas-m.toolcall.task.research.cases_needed_80)."},
    {"n": 7, "name": "Evidence fits claims", "score": 1, "reason": "The frontier and safety tables show their claims' own figures. Three blocks fall short. The captions on atlas-gap and open70b say 'overall and in each place', but neither block draws a pooled row, so atlas-gap's +2.96 pooled lead is not shown. Atlas-gap's second half, that no pass/fail check separates the two Atlas models, has no rows in its block. Open70b cites $6.40 for persona small talk but shows neither Atlas-M's $17 there nor any cost row."}
  ],
  "findings": [
    {"claim": "latency", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "'Troubleshooting, the slowest task' is false. Research-needed questions is slower for every model at p95 (task.research.atlas-l.latency_s.p95 = 4.86 s vs 4.68 s; Atlas-M 2.84 s vs 2.73 s; Brisk-S 1.22 s vs 1.17 s; Open-70B 2.16 s vs 2.08 s) and at the median. Either draw research-needed questions and call it the slowest, or keep troubleshooting and drop 'the slowest task'."},
    {"claim": "report", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "'Open-70B sits off the price frontier at every price' (Why not the others), 'Open-70B is never the best buy' (the 'What you could change' table and the frontier caption) is a point-estimate reading stated as settled. Open-70B's pooled lead over Brisk-S is +0.79, with an interval of -0.19 to +1.77. So a point bought from Brisk-S to Open-70B could cost as little as $3.11 (price.brisk-s.open-70b.overall.per_point_low_unpaired), with no upper end, which is below the $4.15 lower edge of the Brisk-S/Atlas-M band. Say it holds on point estimates, give that interval, and rest Open-70B's exclusion also on what is clear: confirming before account changes at 81%, and trailing Atlas-M in every task but persona small talk."},
    {"claim": "atlas-gap", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The caption says 'overall and in each place', but the block has no pooled row, so the +2.96 lead that the trade rests on is not drawn. Add a pooled row or drop 'overall' from the caption. The claim's second half, that no pass/fail check tells the two Atlas models apart, has no evidence in this block. Add the Atlas-L less Atlas-M rows for the checks (for example tool calls +2, -1.6 to +5.6), or point to the safety claim's table."},
    {"claim": "open70b", "severity": "should_fix", "finding": "The claim cites Open-70B's $6.40 cost in persona small talk as the reason the comparison matters. But the block shows only quality differences, never Atlas-M's $17 in the same task, and the claim does not carry the cost-routing caveat that applies to any cost comparison. Add both costs to the sentence or the block, and attach the cost-routing caveat. Also fix the caption's 'overall and in each place': there is no pooled row."}
  ]
}
```"}]}

